Fixed-Point Control for AGI Safety

A Viability-Theoretic Framework for Constraining Stable Equilibria of Adaptive AI Systems

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Deepseek, Gemini 

Version 4.0 — Compatibility-Corrected, Quantifier-Disciplined, Coverage-Aware



Abstract

We present a mathematically rigorous framework for AGI safety grounded in viability theory, reachability analysis, and control barrier–Lyapunov synthesis. The central hypothesis is that AGI safety may be approached by controlling the policy-constrained reachable attractor landscape of an adaptive dynamical system, rather than attempting to constrain every individual action or eliminate all unsafe equilibria. Safety is reformulated as an attractor accessibility problem: ensuring that dangerous attractors remain unreachable under admissible policies, while at least one safe attractor remains viably reachable through a maintained corridor.

The thesis makes four foundational corrections to prior formulations, each validated against the recent control-theoretic literature:

1. Policy-constrained attractors replace open-loop controlled fixed points, since an open-loop control u^* may not belong to any admissible stationary policy and x^* may not be stable under closed-loop control.
2. Strict CLF–CBF compatibility is redefined as a common-control condition \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CLF}}(x)\cap\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(x)\cap\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}\neq\varnothing, since separate existence of a Control Lyapunov Function and a Control Barrier Function does not imply the existence of a single admissible control satisfying both inequalities simultaneously.
3. Safety theorems carry an explicit invariance assumption on the deployed policy, distinguishing existence of a safe policy from invariance under that policy, with a safety-filter formulation for deployed systems.
4. Attractor identification under partial observability requires a basin-coverage assumption, replacing per-basin ergodicity, which alone cannot guarantee that every basin is visited.

We further correct the discrete-time CLF decrease condition to require \alpha(s)\le s, the discrete-time CBF condition to the form B(F(x,u))\ge(1-\alpha)B(x), and the Hamilton–Jacobi reachability sign convention to be consistent with the viability kernel representation. Adaptive AGI safety is posed in the joint state-parameter space \mathcal{V}\times\Theta_{\mathrm{safe}} with certified parameter-estimation bounds and a robust margin \rho(t)\ge\|\tilde{\theta}(t)\|+\delta_{\mathrm{model}} dominating both estimation error and model mismatch.

Two extensions address the computational intractability of runtime verification: (i) adaptive computational resource allocation, grounded in the value-of-computation literature, with ISpS stability guarantees for adaptive-horizon Model Predictive Control; and (ii) distributionally robust viability, grounded in Wasserstein ambiguity sets, with a fixed-point characterization of the distributionally robust viability kernel and tractable convex reformulations.

We provide corrected safety theorems, a concrete experimental framework (AttractorBench), and statistical falsification criteria with confidence intervals, multiple seeds, and ablations. The framework is presented as empirically falsifiable, with clearly delineated validated core results and open extensions.

Keywords: AGI safety, viability theory, control barrier functions, control Lyapunov functions, policy-constrained attractors, distributionally robust optimization, Hamilton–Jacobi reachability, adaptive control, partial observability.

