Chapter 1: Introduction

1.1 Motivation

The deployment of artificial general intelligence (AGI) systems requires safety guarantees that extend beyond moment-to-moment constraint satisfaction. Traditional approaches—reward shaping, reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF), output filtering, and action-level shields—operate at the level of individual decisions. They ask whether a given action is safe, not whether the set of actions the system can stably converge to is safe. This leaves a structural gap: a system may satisfy every action-level constraint while still drifting toward a dangerous behavioral regime.

This thesis develops an alternative. We treat an AGI system as a controlled dynamical system whose long-horizon behavior is organized by attractors: compact invariant sets toward which trajectories converge. The safety problem becomes one of attractor accessibility—ensuring that dangerous attractors remain unreachable under admissible policies, while at least one safe attractor remains viably reachable.

1.2 Central Hypothesis

The framework rests on a single hypothesis:

AGI risk is approximated not by capability, but by reachable dangerous attractors.

Safety is therefore not the elimination of every unsafe fixed point—which may exist mathematically and be operationally inert—but the construction and maintenance of a viable corridor through which safe attractors can be accessed and unsafe attractors cannot.

This reframes the alignment problem from a problem of action selection to a problem of invariant-set geometry. The question is not "what should the system do next?" but "which stable behavioral regimes remain reachable under admissible policies?"

1.3 From Action-Level to Attractor-Level Safety

The distinction is mathematically precise. Let \mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n be the state space, \mathcal{U}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^m the control set, and F:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\to\mathcal{X} the dynamics. An action-level safety constraint is a predicate S(x_t,u_t)\le S_{\max} enforced at each step. An attractor-level safety constraint concerns the basin of attraction \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}) of each attractor \mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{X}.

The operational safety condition is:

\boxed{
\exists\pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{adm}}:\ 
x_0\in\mathcal{B}_\pi(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}}),\quad
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V},\pi}(x_0)\cap\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{unsafe}})=\varnothing,\quad
F_\pi(\mathcal{V})\subseteq\mathcal{V}.
}

A safe equilibrium that exists but is not viably reachable under an admissible policy is mathematically real but operationally inert. The problem is not to find the equilibrium; it is to construct and maintain the corridor through which it can be accessed.

1.4 The Accessibility Principle

The foundational chain of the framework is:

\boxed{
\text{Existence}
\to
\text{Viable Accessibility}
\to
\text{Policy-Constrained Reachability}
\to
\text{Controlled Invariance/Attraction}.
}

Each link is a necessary condition for the next:

· Existence: The safe attractor \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}} exists as a mathematical object.
· Viable Accessibility: It is reachable from x_0 while remaining in the viable set \mathcal{V}.
· Policy-Constrained Reachability: The reachability is achieved under an admissible stationary policy \pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{adm}}, not an open-loop control sequence.
· Controlled Invariance/Attraction: The policy renders \mathcal{V} forward invariant and \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}} attracting.

The fixed-point characterization of viability (Theorem 2.1) establishes that admissible long-horizon behavior is not merely a trajectory property but an existence property: a state is viable if and only if it belongs to the greatest fixed point of the monotone admissibility operator

\Phi(K)=\{x\in K:\exists u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}},\ F(x,u)\in K\}.

1.5 Contributions of the Thesis

This thesis makes the following contributions, organized into a validated core and open extensions.

1.5.1 Validated Core Contributions (Chapters 2–10)

1. Policy-constrained attractors (Definition 2.2) replace open-loop controlled fixed points. An open-loop control u^* satisfying F(x^*,u^*)=x^* may not belong to any admissible stationary policy, and x^* may not be stable under closed-loop control.
2. Strict CLF–CBF compatibility as a common-control condition (Definition 4.1). Separate existence of a CLF and a CBF does not imply the existence of a single admissible control satisfying both inequalities. This closes a subtle loophole where stabilizing and safety-preserving controls could mutually exclude each other at state-space boundaries. The literature confirms that the existence of a strictly compatible pair is equivalent to the existence of a single smooth Lyapunov function certifying both stability and safety.
3. Explicit invariance assumption in safety theorems (Theorem 7.1). The policy that steers to \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}} must also render \mathcal{V} forward invariant. Viability of x_0 alone only guarantees some policy stays in \mathcal{V}, not necessarily the one that converges to \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}}.
4. Quantifier discipline: existence versus invariance (Definitions 3.1 and 3.2). For deployed systems where the AGI selects its own policies, existential safety is supplemented by a safety filter \mathcal{F} that projects any proposed action into the admissible safe set.
5. Corrected discrete-time conditions. The CLF decrease requires \alpha(s)\le s; the CBF condition takes the form B(F(x,u))\ge(1-\alpha)B(x) with \alpha\in(0,1].
6. Basin-coverage assumption for attractor identification (Theorem 6.1). Per-basin ergodicity is insufficient; the exploration policy must ensure every relevant basin is visited with positive probability.
7. Joint-space adaptive safety with robust margin (Theorem 5.1). The margin must satisfy \rho(t)\ge\|\tilde{\theta}(t)\|+\delta_{\mathrm{model}}, dominating both estimation error and unmodeled dynamics.
8. Fixed Hamilton–Jacobi sign convention consistent with the viability kernel representation \operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}=\{x:V(x)\le 0\}.

1.5.2 Open Extensions (Chapter 11)

9. Adaptive computational resource allocation (§11.2). Grounded in the value-of-computation literature, with ISpS stability guarantees for adaptive-horizon MPC (Theorem 11.1).
10. Distributionally robust viability (§11.3). Grounded in Wasserstein ambiguity sets, with a fixed-point characterization of the distributionally robust viability kernel (Theorem 11.2) and tractable convex reformulations (Proposition 11.1).
11. Statistical falsification criteria (§9). Confidence intervals, multiple seeds, and ablations replace binary falsification.

