2. Mathematical Preliminaries

2.1 State, Control, and Dynamics

Let \mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n be the state space, \mathcal{U}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^m the control set, and \mathcal{W}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^p a disturbance set. Consider the discrete-time controlled dynamical system

x_{t+1}=F(x_t,u_t,w_t),\qquad F:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\times\mathcal{W}\to\mathcal{X}.

In the deterministic case, x_{t+1}=F(x_t,u_t). Continuous-time analogues are written \dot{x}=f(x,u). Stochastic systems are represented by a transition kernel P(dx'\mid x,u).

2.2 Policy-Constrained Attractors

Definition 2.1 (Policy Space). Let \Pi be a set of admissible stationary policies \pi:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{U}. The closed-loop map under \pi is

F_\pi(x)=F(x,\pi(x)).

Definition 2.2 (Controlled Attractor). A compact set \mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{X} is a controlled attractor under \pi\in\Pi if:

1. F_\pi(\mathcal{A})\subseteq\mathcal{A} (invariance);
2. There exists an open neighborhood \mathcal{N} of \mathcal{A} such that
   \lim_{t\to\infty}\operatorname{dist}(x_t,\mathcal{A})=0
   for all x_0\in\mathcal{N}.

The basin of attraction is

\mathcal{B}_\pi(\mathcal{A})=\left\{x_0\in\mathcal{X}:\lim_{t\to\infty}\operatorname{dist}(x_t,\mathcal{A})=0\right\}.

Remark 2.1. This definition replaces the open-loop controlled fixed point (x^*,u^*) satisfying F(x^*,u^*)=x^*. An open-loop control u^* may not belong to any admissible stationary policy, and x^* may not be stable under closed-loop control. Policy-constrained attractors are the correct objects for safety analysis.

2.3 Viability and Reachability

Let \mathcal{V}\subseteq\mathcal{X} be the viable (safe) set, e.g.

\mathcal{V}=\{x\in\mathcal{X}:S(x)\le S_{\max}\}.

Let \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}\subseteq\mathcal{U} be the set of admissible controls.

Definition 2.3 (Viable Reachability). The viable-reachable set to a target attractor \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}} is

\operatorname{ViableReach}(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}})
=
\left\{
x_0\in\mathcal{V}:
\exists\pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{adm}},\ 
x_t\in\mathcal{V}\ \forall t\ge 0,\ 
\operatorname{dist}(x_t,\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}})\to 0
\right\}.

Definition 2.4 (Viability Kernel). The viability kernel of \mathcal{V} is

\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}
=
\left\{
x\in\mathcal{V}:
\exists u_0,u_1,\dots\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}
\text{ such that } x_t\in\mathcal{V}\ \forall t\ge 0
\right\}.

Theorem 2.1 (Fixed-Point Characterization of Viability). Let \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X}) be the powerset lattice of \mathcal{X}, ordered by inclusion. Define the monotone admissibility operator

\Phi(K)=\{x\in K:\exists u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}},\ F(x,u)\in K\}.

Then \Phi is monotone, and its greatest fixed point coincides exactly with the viability kernel:

\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}=\operatorname{gfp}(\Phi).

Proof sketch. By the Knaster–Tarski fixed-point theorem, every monotone operator on a complete lattice has a greatest fixed point. The iterative algorithm K_0=\mathcal{V}, K_{i+1}=\Phi(K_i) generates a nested sequence of closed sets converging to \operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}. \square

Remark 2.2. This result establishes that admissible long-horizon behavior is not merely a trajectory property but an existence property: a state is viable if and only if it belongs to the greatest fixed point of the admissibility operator.

2.4 The Accessibility Principle (Corrected)

The original chain

\text{Existence}\to\text{Viable Accessibility}\to\text{Reachability}\to\text{Stability}

is replaced by:

\boxed{
\text{Existence}
\to
\text{Viable Accessibility}
\to
\text{Policy-Constrained Reachability}
\to
\text{Controlled Invariance/Attraction}.
}

The operational condition is:

\boxed{
x_0\in\operatorname{ViableReach}(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}}).
}

A safe equilibrium that exists but is not viably reachable under an admissible policy is mathematically real but operationally inert.

