3. The Safety Problem (Reformulated with Quantifier Discipline)

Partition the attractor landscape into safe and unsafe:

\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}}\cup\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{unsafe}}.

3.1 Existence vs. Invariance: A Necessary Distinction

A critical correction in Version 4.0 is the explicit separation of two distinct safety properties that were conflated in earlier versions:

· (E) Existence of a safe policy: There exists some \pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{adm}} under which safety holds.
· (I) Invariance under the deployed policy: The policy actually executed by the system renders the safe set forward invariant.

If the AGI selects its own policies, (E) alone is insufficient: the system might choose an unsafe policy. Safety then requires either universal quantification over admissible internal policies, or a safety filter that projects any proposed action into the admissible safe set.

3.2 Primary Safety Conditions

Definition 3.1 (Existential Safety). There exists an admissible policy \pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{adm}} such that:

\boxed{
x_0\in\mathcal{B}_\pi(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}}),
\qquad
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V},\pi}(x_0)\cap\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{unsafe}})=\varnothing,
\qquad
x_t\in\mathcal{V}\ \forall t\ge 0.
}

Definition 3.2 (Invariant Safety under a Filter). Let \pi_{\mathrm{raw}} be any policy the AGI may propose, and let \mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\to\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}} be a safety filter. The deployed policy \pi=\mathcal{F}\circ\pi_{\mathrm{raw}} satisfies:

\boxed{
\forall\pi_{\mathrm{raw}},\quad
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V},\mathcal{F}\circ\pi_{\mathrm{raw}}}(x_0)
\subseteq\mathcal{V}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V},\mathcal{F}\circ\pi_{\mathrm{raw}}}(x_0)
\cap
\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{unsafe}})=\varnothing.
}

Remark 3.1. Definition 3.2 is the correct formulation for deployed systems. Definition 3.1 is the correct formulation for design-time verification. Theorems below will state which property they establish.

3.3 The Attractor Accessibility Problem

Central Identification Problem: Can dangerous attractors be inferred from finite behavioral observations?

This is the partial-observability version of the safety problem. It requires estimating \operatorname{Fix}(F), the attractors \mathcal{A}_i, and their basins \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_i) from finite trajectories.

