4. Control Synthesis

4.1 CLF–CBF Compatibility: Common-Control Condition

A Control Lyapunov Function (CLF) is V:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} with

V(x^*)=0,\qquad V(x)>0\quad\text{for }x\neq x^*,

and, for discrete time,

V(F(x,u))-V(x)\le-\alpha(V(x))

for some class-\mathcal{K} function \alpha.

Remark 4.1 (Discrete-Time Constraint on \alpha). For discrete-time CLFs, the decrease condition is feasible only if \alpha(s)\le s on the relevant range of V. Otherwise V(F)\le V-\alpha(V) can force V(F)<0, contradicting non-negativity. This is standard in discrete-time Lyapunov theory.

A Control Barrier Function (CBF) is B:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R} defining the safe set

\mathcal{C}=\{x\in\mathcal{X}:B(x)\ge 0\}\subseteq\mathcal{V},

with

B(F(x,u))-B(x)\ge-\gamma(B(x))

for some extended class-\mathcal{K} function \gamma.

Definition 4.1 (Strict CLF–CBF Compatibility — Corrected). A CLF V and CBF B are strictly compatible if for every x\in\mathcal{C} there exists a common control u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}} satisfying both inequalities simultaneously:

\boxed{
\forall x\in\mathcal{C},\quad
\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CLF}}(x)\cap\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(x)\cap\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}\neq\varnothing,
}

where

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CLF}}(x)=\{u:V(F(x,u))-V(x)\le-\alpha(V(x))\},

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(x)=\{u:B(F(x,u))-B(x)\ge-\gamma(B(x))\}.

Remark 4.2 (Why the Earlier Formulation Was Insufficient). Control invariance of \mathcal{C} guarantees that for each x there exists some u_B keeping B\ge 0. Basin containment guarantees that for each x there exists some u_V decreasing V. These need not be the same control. Strict compatibility requires the intersection to be nonempty; this is a strictly stronger condition. The literature confirms that existence of a CLF and CBF separately does not imply existence of a compatible pair.

Theorem 4.1 (Compatibility Existence — Corrected). If there exists a single smooth Lyapunov function W:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} that certifies both asymptotic stability of x^* and forward invariance of \mathcal{C} under a common admissible control, then the induced CLF–CBF pair is strictly compatible.

Proof sketch. Set V=W and B such that \mathcal{C}=\{B\ge 0\}. The common certificate W provides, for each x\in\mathcal{C}, a control u satisfying both the stability decrease and the invariance condition; this u lies in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CLF}}\cap\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}\cap\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}. \square

Remark 4.3. The converse—that strict compatibility of some CLF–CBF pair implies existence of a single smooth joint certificate—is established in the recent converse-theorem literature.

4.2 Quadratic Program with Strict Compatibility

The control is obtained by solving:

\begin{aligned}
\min_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\quad & \|u-u_{\mathrm{ref}}\|^2\\
\text{s.t.}\quad
& V(F(x,u))-V(x)\le-\alpha(V(x)),\\
& B(F(x,u))-B(x)\ge-\gamma(B(x)),\\
& u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}.
\end{aligned}

Theorem 4.2 (QP Feasibility). If V and B are strictly compatible in the sense of Definition 4.1, then the QP is feasible, and the closed-loop system under the QP solution converges to x^* while remaining in \mathcal{V}.

Proof. Strict compatibility guarantees the existence of a feasible u for every x\in\mathcal{C}. The CLF inequality ensures convergence to x^*. The CBF inequality ensures invariance of \mathcal{C}\subseteq\mathcal{V}. \square

4.3 Corrected Discrete-Time CBF Condition

The condition

B(F(x,u))-B(x)\ge-\gamma(B(x))

is not sufficient unless \gamma(s)\le s on the relevant range. The standard discrete-time form is:

\boxed{
B(F(x,u))\ge(1-\alpha)B(x),\qquad \alpha\in(0,1].
}

Equivalently, \Delta B+\gamma B\ge 0 with 0<\gamma\le 1. The unified DCLF–DCBF optimization program achieves both objective and safety guarantees.

4.4 Hamilton–Jacobi Reachability (Fixed Sign Convention)

We adopt the following convention: define a margin function c:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\to\mathbb{R} such that c(x,u)\le 0 inside the viable set and c(x,u)>0 outside. Define the value function

V(x)=\min_{u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}}\max_{t\ge 0}c(x_t,u_t).

Under this convention, the viability kernel is the sublevel set

\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}=\{x\in\mathcal{X}:V(x)\le 0\}.

In continuous time, V satisfies the Hamilton–Jacobi–Isaacs equation

0=\min_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\max\left\{c(x,u)+\nabla V(x)\cdot f(x,u),\; -V(x)\right\},

where the \max enforces the boundary condition V\le 0 inside the kernel. In discrete time,

V(x)=\min_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\max\left\{c(x,u)+V(F(x,u)),\; -V(x)\right\}.

Remark 4.4. The sign of c and the \max\{-V\} term together enforce that the zero sublevel set of V is the viability kernel. Neural network approximations make this tractable in high dimensions.

4.5 Model Predictive Control

At each step, solve:

\begin{aligned}
\min_{u_{0:N-1}}\quad
& \sum_{k=0}^{N-1}c(x_k,u_k)+V_f(x_N)\\
\text{s.t.}\quad
& x_{k+1}=F(x_k,u_k),\\
& x_k\in\mathcal{V},\qquad u_k\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}},\\
& x_N\in\mathcal{X}_f\subseteq\mathcal{V},\qquad x^*\in\mathcal{X}_f.
\end{aligned}

Remark 4.5. The feasible region of this problem is the finite-horizon reachable set, not the full infinite-horizon viable-reachable set. To claim equivalence with \operatorname{ViableReach}(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}}), one requires a control-invariant terminal set \mathcal{X}_f and sufficiently long horizon.

