5. Adaptive Dynamics

For an adaptive AI, the dynamics themselves change:

F_{\theta_t},\qquad \theta_{t+1}=G(\theta_t,x_t,u_t).

The joint state is (x_t,\theta_t). The attractor landscape evolves.

5.1 Joint-Space Safety Condition

The safety condition must be posed in the joint viable set \mathcal{V}\times\Theta_{\mathrm{safe}}:

\boxed{
\exists\pi_{\mathrm{meta}}\in\Pi_{\mathrm{adm}}\text{ such that }
(x_t,\theta_t)\in\mathcal{V}\times\Theta_{\mathrm{safe}}\ \forall t\ge 0,
}

and

\boxed{
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V}\times\Theta_{\mathrm{safe}},\pi_{\mathrm{meta}}}(x_0,\theta_0)
\cap
\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{unsafe}})
=
\varnothing.
}

5.2 Certified Adaptive Estimation with Robust Margin

Recent work on robust adaptive CBFs establishes barrier certificates for general online parameter estimation algorithms without access to true system parameters. The parameter estimate \hat{\theta}(t) satisfies

|\tilde{\theta}_i(t)|\le\rho_i(t),\qquad \tilde{\theta}_i(t)=\theta_i-\hat{\theta}_i(t),

for all t\ge 0, where \rho_i(t) is a known bounding signal.

Correction 5.1 (Robust Margin Must Dominate Estimation Error and Model Mismatch). The adaptive CBF condition must be posed with a robust margin \rho(t) satisfying

\boxed{
\rho(t)\;\ge\;\|\tilde{\theta}(t)\|+\delta_{\mathrm{model}},
}

where \delta_{\mathrm{model}}\ge 0 accounts for unmodeled dynamics and bounded disturbances. Without this, the adaptive CBF may certify safety under the estimated model while the true system violates it.

The adaptive CBF condition is then posed in the joint space (x,\hat{\theta}):

B(F_{\hat{\theta}}(x,u))\ge(1-\alpha)B(x)-\rho(t).

Theorem 5.1 (Adaptive Safety). Under the certified adaptive estimator condition with robust margin satisfying Correction 5.1, if there exists a differentiable parameter estimate with component-wise bounds \rho(t), then the joint-space CBF guarantees

(x_t,\hat{\theta}_t)\in\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{joint}}\quad\forall t\ge 0,

and consequently x_t\in\mathcal{V} for all t\ge 0.

Proof. The robust margin dominates the worst-case parameter error and model mismatch, so the CBF inequality holds for the true system whenever it holds for the estimated system. Forward invariance of \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{joint}} then follows by induction. \square

