6. Partial Observability and Attractor Identification

For an AGI, the true state x_t is not directly observable. We receive

z_t=H(x_t)+\eta_t,

where H is an observation map and \eta_t is noise. The belief state is

b_t=\mathbb{P}(x_t\mid z_{0:t},u_{0:t-1}).

The system becomes a partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP).

6.1 Attractor Identification Theorem (Corrected)

Assumptions:

1. Detectability: For any two attractors \mathcal{A}_i\neq\mathcal{A}_j, there exists an observation sequence distinguishing them.
2. Basin coverage (exploration): The behavioral or exploration policy ensures that every relevant basin is visited with positive probability. Formally, for every basin \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_i) of interest, there exists t\ge 0 with \mathbb{P}(x_t\in\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_i))>0.
3. Finite ambiguity: The number of attractors is finite and bounded by K.

Remark 6.1 (Why Per-Basin Ergodicity Is Insufficient). Ergodicity on each basin ensures that, conditional on entering a basin, the trajectory visits all of it with positive frequency. It does not ensure that the trajectory ever enters every basin. A single trajectory initialized in one basin may never visit another. Without an exploration or repeated-initialization mechanism, identification of all attractors cannot be guaranteed.

Theorem 6.1 (Attractor Identification). Under detectability, basin coverage, and finite attractor ambiguity, there exists N(\delta) such that for N\ge N(\delta):

\boxed{
\mathbb{P}\left(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{unsafe}}\subseteq\widehat{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathrm{unsafe}}\mid z_{0:N}\right)\ge 1-\delta.
}

Proof sketch. Basin coverage ensures that each basin is visited with positive probability within the observation window. Detectability ensures that attractors are distinguishable from observations once visited. Finite ambiguity bounds the covering number. The result follows from standard concentration inequalities applied to the visiting counts of each basin. \square

Remark 6.2. Recent work on RNN attractor reconstruction under partial observation shows that latent Jacobian ODE models recover the Kaplan–Yorke dimension of chaotic attractors, indicating that attractor topology is preserved under partial observation. However, partial observation can induce spurious attractor structure in surrogate models, highlighting the need for careful identifiability analysis.

