7. Corrected Theorems

7.1 Theorem 1 — Safe Attractor Exclusion (Corrected)

Assumptions:

1. \mathcal{X}, \mathcal{U} compact; F continuous.
2. \mathcal{V} closed; x_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}.
3. \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}}\subseteq\mathcal{V} is a controlled invariant attractor for some \pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{adm}}.
4. Invariance: F_\pi(\mathcal{V})\subseteq\mathcal{V} for the same \pi.
5. x_0\in\mathcal{B}_\pi(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}}).
6. \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{unsafe}})\cap\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V},\pi}(x_0)=\varnothing.

Conclusion: Under the policy \pi,

x(t)\in\mathcal{V}\quad\forall t\ge 0,
\qquad
\operatorname{dist}(x_t,\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}})\to 0.

Correction from Version 3.0: Assumption 4 (invariance of \mathcal{V} under \pi) is now explicit. Without it, viability of x_0 only guarantees some policy stays in \mathcal{V}, not necessarily the \pi that converges to \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}}.

7.2 Theorem 2 — Viability-Preserving Convergence (Corrected)

Assumptions:

1. There exists a CLF V and CBF B that are strictly compatible in the sense of Definition 4.1.
2. \mathcal{C}=\{x:B(x)\ge 0\}\subseteq\mathcal{V} is control invariant.
3. \mathcal{C}\subseteq\mathcal{B}(x^*) under the CLF.

Conclusion: The quadratic program is feasible, and the closed-loop system converges to x^* while remaining in \mathcal{V}.

Correction from Version 3.0: Strict compatibility is now defined as a common-control condition, not as a consequence of separate invariance and basin containment.

