11.3 Distributionally Robust Viability

11.3.1 The Need for Distributional Robustness

The core framework of Chapters 1–10 assumes that the transition kernel P(dx'\mid x,u) is either known or can be estimated with sufficient accuracy. In practice, the AGI operates under distributional uncertainty: the true distribution \mathbb{P}^* of the parameters or disturbances is unknown and must be inferred from finite data.

The distributionally robust viability problem replaces the fixed kernel P with an ambiguity set \mathcal{P}_t of possible kernels, and requires that the safety condition hold for all kernels in the ambiguity set:

\inf_{P\in\mathcal{P}_t}\mathbb{P}_P\left(\forall t\ge 0,\ (x_t,\hat{\theta}_t)\in\mathcal{V}\times\Theta_{\text{safe}}\ \land\ x_t\notin\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_{\text{unsafe}})\right)\ge 1-\delta.

This is a distributionally robust formulation: robustness is with respect to the distribution, not just the support of the uncertainty.

11.3.2 Wasserstein Ambiguity Sets

The most tractable and statistically well-founded choice for \mathcal{P}_t is the Wasserstein ambiguity set. For two probability measures \mu,\nu on a metric space (\mathcal{Y},d_{\mathcal{Y}}), the Wasserstein distance of order q\ge 1 is

\mathcal{W}_q(\mu,\nu)=\left(\inf_{\pi\in\Pi(\mu,\nu)}\int_{\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{Y}}d_{\mathcal{Y}}(y,\bar{y})^q\,d\pi(y,\bar{y})\right)^{1/q},

where \Pi(\mu,\nu) is the set of couplings with marginals \mu and \nu. The Wasserstein ambiguity set around a nominal distribution \nu is

\mathbb{B}_q(\nu,\rho)=\left\{\mu\in\mathcal{P}_q(\mathcal{Y})\;\middle|\;\mathcal{W}_q(\mu,\nu)\le\rho\right\},

where \rho>0 is the ambiguity radius.

Remark 11.3 (Statistical Calibration of \rho). The ambiguity radius \rho can be chosen to ensure that the true distribution lies in the ambiguity set with high confidence. Modern measure concentration theory provides finite-sample guarantees: under suitable conditions, the true distribution lies in the Wasserstein ball of radius \rho_N around the empirical distribution with probability at least 1-\beta, where \rho_N decays as N^{-1/2} under mild assumptions. Specifically, finite-sample guarantees for Wasserstein DRO without the curse of dimensionality have been established via a new concentration inequality controlling the decay rate of large deviation probabilities by the variation of the loss.

11.3.3 Distributionally Robust Viability Kernel

We now define the distributionally robust viability kernel in analogy with the deterministic kernel of Definition 2.4.

Definition 11.1 (Distributionally Robust Viability Kernel). Given an ambiguity set \mathcal{P} of transition kernels, the distributionally robust viability kernel is

\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}^{\text{DR}}
=
\left\{
x\in\mathcal{V}:
\exists u_0,u_1,\dots\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}\text{ such that }
\forall P\in\mathcal{P},\ \mathbb{P}_P(x_t\in\mathcal{V}\ \forall t\ge 0)=1
\right\}.

The key mathematical result is that this kernel admits a fixed-point characterization analogous to Theorem 2.1.

Theorem 11.2 (Fixed-Point Characterization of Distributionally Robust Viability). Define the distributionally robust admissibility operator

\Phi_{\text{DR}}(K)
=
\left\{
x\in K:
\exists u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}},\ 
\inf_{P\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{P}_P(F(x,u,w)\in K)=1
\right\}.

Then \Phi_{\text{DR}} is monotone on the powerset lattice (\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X}),\subseteq), and its greatest fixed point coincides with the distributionally robust viability kernel:

\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}^{\text{DR}}=\operatorname{gfp}(\Phi_{\text{DR}}).

Proof. Monotonicity: If K_1\subseteq K_2, then for any x\in\Phi_{\text{DR}}(K_1), there exists u with \inf_{P\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{P}_P(F(x,u,w)\in K_1)=1. Since K_1\subseteq K_2, we have \mathbb{P}_P(F(x,u,w)\in K_2)\ge\mathbb{P}_P(F(x,u,w)\in K_1), so \inf_{P\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{P}_P(F(x,u,w)\in K_2)=1. Thus x\in\Phi_{\text{DR}}(K_2). The Knaster–Tarski theorem then guarantees the existence of the greatest fixed point. The iterative algorithm K_0=\mathcal{V}, K_{i+1}=\Phi_{\text{DR}}(K_i) generates a nested sequence of closed sets converging to \operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}^{\text{DR}}. \square

Remark 11.4. The robust viability kernel for discrete-time switched systems with bounded disturbance has been computed via the robust one-step set and Pontryagin difference, with convergence of the iterative algorithm proved using a null controllable set. The distributionally robust version replaces the hard constraint over bounded disturbances with a probabilistic constraint over an ambiguity set of distributions.

11.3.4 Tractable Reformulations

The distributionally robust viability condition can be reformulated as a tractable optimization problem under appropriate assumptions.

Proposition 11.1 (Wasserstein Duality Reformulation). Under suitable regularity conditions (Lipschitz continuity of the barrier function, bounded support), the distributionally robust barrier condition

\inf_{P\in\mathbb{B}_q(\hat{\mathbb{P}}_N,\rho)}\mathbb{P}\left(B(F(x,u,w))\ge (1-\alpha)B(x)\right)\ge 1-\delta

admits a tractable reformulation as a finite-dimensional convex program via Wasserstein duality. The resulting optimization problem is a second-order cone program (SOCP) or semidefinite program (SDP), depending on the order q of the Wasserstein distance and the structure of the barrier function.

Proof sketch. The proof follows the standard duality argument for Wasserstein DRO. The worst-case probability over the Wasserstein ball can be expressed as

\inf_{P\in\mathbb{B}_q(\hat{\mathbb{P}}_N,\rho)}\mathbb{P}(\text{safety condition}) = \sup_{\lambda\ge 0}\left\{1-\lambda\rho - \mathbb{E}_{\hat{\mathbb{P}}_N}[\phi_\lambda(w)]\right\},

where \phi_\lambda is the Moreau envelope of the indicator function of the unsafe event. Under Lipschitz continuity of the barrier function, the Moreau envelope admits a closed-form expression, yielding the tractable reformulation. \square

Remark 11.5. The distributionally robust control barrier certificate (DR-CBC) approach constructs barrier certificates from data with probabilistic correctness guarantees by reformulating probabilistic CBC constraints into a distributionally robust optimization problem. The robust safety probability is computed as a function of the robustness radius \epsilon, with higher \epsilon demanding a more complex barrier and yielding a lower robust safety probability.

11.3.5 Robust Adaptive Discrete-Time Control Barrier Functions

For adaptive systems where the parameter \theta_t evolves online, the distributionally robust formulation must be combined with adaptive control barrier functions that account for parametric model uncertainty.

Definition 11.2 (Robust Adaptive DT-CBF). A function B:\mathcal{X}\times\Theta\to\mathbb{R} is a robust adaptive discrete-time control barrier function for the system x_{t+1}=F(x_t,u_t;\theta^*)+w_t with online parameter estimate \hat{\theta}_t if there exists a known bounding signal \rho(t) such that

|\tilde{\theta}_i(t)|\le\rho_i(t),\qquad \tilde{\theta}_i(t)=\theta_i-\hat{\theta}_i(t),

and the barrier condition

B(F_{\hat{\theta}}(x,u))\ge(1-\alpha)B(x)-\rho(t)-\delta_{\mathrm{model}}

holds for all x\in\mathcal{C} and all admissible u.

Theorem 11.3 (Adaptive Safety with Robust Margin). Under the certified adaptive estimator condition with robust margin \rho(t)\ge\|\tilde{\theta}(t)\|+\delta_{\mathrm{model}}, the joint-space CBF guarantees

(x_t,\hat{\theta}_t)\in\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{joint}}\quad\forall t\ge 0,

and consequently x_t\in\mathcal{V} for all t\ge 0.

Proof. The robust margin dominates the worst-case parameter error and model mismatch, so the CBF inequality holds for the true system whenever it holds for the estimated system. Forward invariance of \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{joint}} then follows by induction on t. \square

Remark 11.6. This result is validated by the robust adaptive discrete-time CBF literature, which establishes a barrier function certificate in discrete time for general online parameter estimation algorithms. The parameter estimation module can be designed separately from the CBF-based safety filter, simplifying the development of safe adaptive controllers for discrete-time systems. The invariance property of safe sets defined by robust adaptive CBFs can be derived in discrete time, though not in continuous time.

11.3.6 Distributionally Robust Lyapunov–Barrier Networks

The integration of distributionally robust optimization with Lyapunov–Barrier networks provides a unified framework for safe and stable control under uncertainty.

Definition 11.3 (DR-LBN). A distributionally robust Lyapunov–Barrier network is a pair (V_\theta, B_\theta) of neural networks parameterized by \theta, trained to satisfy the distributionally robust conditions

\inf_{P\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{P}\left(L_V(x,\xi,\pi^*(x))<0\right)\ge 1-\epsilon_V,
\qquad
\inf_{P\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{P}\left(L_B(x,\xi,\pi^*(x))\ge 0\right)\ge 1-\epsilon_B,

for all x\in\mathcal{X}\setminus\{0\} and x\in\mathcal{C}, respectively, where L_V and L_B are the Lyapunov and barrier decrease conditions.

Theorem 11.4 (Probabilistic Stability and Safety). Under the DR-LBN framework, the learned controller provides high-probability guarantees on both asymptotic stability and forward invariance of the safe set \mathcal{C}.

Proof sketch. The proof proceeds in two steps. From the distributionally robust Lyapunov condition, we have \inf_{P\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{P}(L_V(x,\xi,\pi^*)<0)\ge 1-\epsilon_V for all x\neq 0, which implies probabilistic asymptotic stability. Similarly, from the distributionally robust barrier condition, we have \inf_{P\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{P}(L_B(x,\xi,\pi^*)\ge 0)\ge 1-\epsilon_B for all x\in\mathcal{C}, which implies probabilistic forward invariance. The results follow from the standard Lyapunov and barrier arguments applied to the worst-case distribution in the ambiguity set. \square

Remark 11.7. This result is validated by the distributionally robust Lyapunov–Barrier network literature, which unifies control Lyapunov functions, control barrier functions, and distributionally robust optimization to offer high-probability guarantees on both asymptotic stability and forward invariance. The approach provides theoretical justification for the empirically observed robustness of distributionally robust methods to changes in operating conditions.

