11.5 Open Problems and Limitations

The extensions presented in this chapter are mathematically grounded but leave several open problems:

1. Sample complexity of distributionally robust viability. While finite-sample guarantees for Wasserstein DRO exist, their application to the viability kernel requires bounding the complexity of the value function and the barrier function. The sample complexity of learning the distributionally robust viability kernel remains an open problem.
2. Tightness of the robust margin. The robust margin \rho(t)+\delta_{\mathrm{model}} must dominate the worst-case estimation error. In practice, this may be conservative. Adaptive schemes that tighten the margin as the parameter estimate improves are needed.
3. Computational complexity of the marginal value. The marginal value \mathcal{M}\text{-Val} is defined as a derivative of a probability with respect to computational budget. Estimating this derivative at runtime is itself computationally expensive. Approximations via finite differences or surrogate models are needed.
4. Integration with partial observability. The distributionally robust formulation assumes that the state is observed (possibly with noise) and that the ambiguity is over the transition kernel. In the POMDP setting, the ambiguity is over the observation model as well, and the belief state b_t must be included in the viability analysis.
5. Empirical validation. The adaptive computation allocation and distributionally robust viability extensions have not been empirically validated in the AttractorBench framework. Systematic experiments comparing uniform vs. adaptive computation, and nominal vs. distributionally robust safety, are needed.

