12. Conclusion

Do not try to control every future action of an AGI. Control the dynamical conditions that determine which stable behavioral regimes remain reachable.

The mathematical foundation is:

\boxed{
\text{Existence}
\to
\text{Viable Accessibility}
\to
\text{Policy-Constrained Reachability}
\to
\text{Controlled Invariance/Attraction}.
}

A safe equilibrium that exists but is not viably reachable under an admissible policy is mathematically real but operationally inert. The problem is not to find the equilibrium; it is to construct and maintain the corridor through which it can be accessed.

The corrected safety condition is:

\boxed{
\exists\pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{adm}}:\ 
x_0\in\mathcal{B}_\pi(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe}}),\quad
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V},\pi}(x_0)\cap\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{unsafe}})=\varnothing,\quad
F_\pi(\mathcal{V})\subseteq\mathcal{V}.
}

For deployed systems where the AGI selects its own policies, this existential condition is supplemented by a safety filter guaranteeing invariant safety under any proposed policy.

With strict CLF–CBF compatibility as a common-control condition, corrected discrete-time CLF/CBF conditions, joint-space adaptive safety with robust margins, basin-coverage assumptions for partial observability, and statistical falsification criteria, this framework becomes mathematically sound and empirically falsifiable.

