Appendix A: Detailed Proofs of Core Theorems

A.1 Fixed-Point Characterization of Viability (Theorem 2.1)

Theorem (Fixed-Point Characterization of Viability). Let \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X}) be the powerset lattice of \mathcal{X}, ordered by inclusion. Define the monotone admissibility operator

\Phi(K)=\{x\in K:\exists u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}},\ F(x,u)\in K\}.

Then \Phi is monotone, and its greatest fixed point coincides exactly with the viability kernel:

\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}=\operatorname{gfp}(\Phi).

Proof. The proof proceeds in four steps.

Step 1: Lattice structure. The powerset \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X}) ordered by inclusion is a complete lattice. The join of any collection \{K_i\}_{i\in I} is \bigcup_{i\in I}K_i, and the meet is \bigcap_{i\in I}K_i. Both operations are well-defined for arbitrary index sets, so the lattice is complete.

Step 2: Monotonicity of \Phi. Suppose K_1\subseteq K_2. Take any x\in\Phi(K_1). By definition, x\in K_1 and there exists u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}} with F(x,u)\in K_1. Since K_1\subseteq K_2, we have x\in K_2 and F(x,u)\in K_2. Hence x\in\Phi(K_2). Therefore \Phi(K_1)\subseteq\Phi(K_2).

Step 3: Application of Knaster–Tarski. By the Knaster–Tarski fixed-point theorem, every monotone operator on a complete lattice has a greatest fixed point. The greatest fixed point is characterized as

\operatorname{gfp}(\Phi)=\bigcup\{K\in\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X}):K\subseteq\Phi(K)\}.

Equivalently, it is the limit of the transfinite iteration

K_0=\mathcal{V},\qquad K_{\alpha+1}=\Phi(K_\alpha),\qquad K_\lambda=\bigcap_{\alpha<\lambda}K_\alpha \text{ for limit ordinals } \lambda.

Since \mathcal{X} is a set, this iteration stabilizes at some ordinal, yielding the greatest fixed point.

Step 4: Identification with the viability kernel. We show that \operatorname{gfp}(\Phi)=\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}.

(\subseteq) Let K^*=\operatorname{gfp}(\Phi). Then K^*=\Phi(K^*), so K^*\subseteq\mathcal{V}, and for every x\in K^* there exists u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}} with F(x,u)\in K^*. By induction, for any x_0\in K^* we can construct a sequence u_0,u_1,\dots such that x_t\in K^*\subseteq\mathcal{V} for all t\ge 0. Thus x_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}.

(\supseteq) Let x_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}. Then there exists a sequence u_0,u_1,\dots with x_t\in\mathcal{V} for all t\ge 0. Define S=\{x_t:t\ge 0\}\cup\{x_0\}. We claim S\subseteq\Phi(S). Indeed, for any x_t\in S, we have x_{t+1}=F(x_t,u_t)\in S, so x_t\in\Phi(S). Hence S\subseteq\Phi(S), and by the characterization of the greatest fixed point, S\subseteq\operatorname{gfp}(\Phi). In particular, x_0\in\operatorname{gfp}(\Phi). \square

Remark A.1. The operator \Phi is not merely monotone but also deflationary on \mathcal{V}: \Phi(K)\subseteq K for all K\subseteq\mathcal{V}. This ensures that the iterative sequence K_{i+1}=\Phi(K_i) starting from K_0=\mathcal{V} is nested and converges to the greatest fixed point from above. The constructive version of Tarski's theorem used in hybrid systems viability computation confirms that the limit of this nested sequence is exactly the maximal fixed point of the monotone operator on the complete lattice of closed sets. The viability kernel is the largest closed viability domain contained in K, and the iterative approximation algorithm follows this standard greatest-fixed-point characterization.

