A.2 Strict CLF–CBF Compatibility: Equivalence with a Single Joint Certificate

Theorem (Converse Control Lyapunov Theorem for Joint Safety and Stability). The existence of a strictly compatible pair of control Lyapunov and control barrier functions is equivalent to the existence of a single smooth Lyapunov function that certifies both asymptotic stability and safety.

Proof sketch. The proof establishes both directions of the equivalence.

(\Leftarrow) Single certificate \Rightarrow strict compatibility. Suppose there exists a single smooth function W:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} with W(x^*)=0, W(x)>0 for x\neq x^*, and such that under a common control u:

W(F(x,u))-W(x)\le-\alpha(W(x)),

and

\mathcal{C}=\{x:W(x)\le c\}\subseteq\mathcal{V}

is forward invariant under the same u. Set V=W and define B(x)=c-W(x) so that \mathcal{C}=\{x:B(x)\ge 0\}. For every x\in\mathcal{C}, the control u satisfying the decrease condition for W simultaneously satisfies:

V(F(x,u))-V(x)\le-\alpha(V(x)),

and

B(F(x,u))-B(x)=W(x)-W(F(x,u))\ge\alpha(W(x))\ge 0,

where the last inequality uses \alpha(W(x))\ge 0. Thus u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CLF}}(x)\cap\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(x)\cap\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}, establishing strict compatibility.

(\Rightarrow) Strict compatibility \Rightarrow single certificate. Suppose a strictly compatible CLF–CBF pair (V,B) exists. The recent converse theorem literature establishes that strict compatibility of a CLF–CBF pair is equivalent to the existence of a single smooth Lyapunov function certifying both asymptotic stability and safety. The characterization is via a partial differential equation (PDE) with prescribed boundary conditions on the safe set, ensuring that the safe set is exactly certified by this Lyapunov function. The result also implies that if a safety and stability specification cannot be certified by a single Lyapunov function, then any pair of control Lyapunov and control barrier functions necessarily leads to a conflict and cannot be satisfied simultaneously in a robust sense. \square

Remark A.2. The counterexample demonstrating that the separate existence of a CLF and a CBF does not imply the existence of a strictly compatible pair is given in the IEEE TAC paper on converse theorems. This formally closes the loophole identified in Version 3.0 of the framework.

