A.3 Adaptive Safety with Robust Margin: Forward Invariance Induction (Theorem 5.1)

Theorem (Adaptive Safety). Under the certified adaptive estimator condition with robust margin satisfying

\rho(t)\ge\|\tilde{\theta}(t)\|+\delta_{\mathrm{model}},

if there exists a differentiable parameter estimate with component-wise bounds \rho(t), then the joint-space CBF guarantees

(x_t,\hat{\theta}_t)\in\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{joint}}\quad\forall t\ge 0,

and consequently x_t\in\mathcal{V} for all t\ge 0.

Proof. The proof is by induction on the discrete time index t.

Base case (t=0). By assumption, the initial state (x_0,\hat{\theta}_0)\in\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{joint}}, since the safe set is defined as

\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{joint}}=\{(x,\hat{\theta}):B(x)\ge 0,\ \hat{\theta}\in\Theta_{\mathrm{safe}}\}.

Inductive step. Suppose (x_t,\hat{\theta}_t)\in\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{joint}}. We must show (x_{t+1},\hat{\theta}_{t+1})\in\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{joint}}. The adaptive CBF condition is posed as

B(F_{\hat{\theta}}(x_t,u_t))\ge(1-\alpha)B(x_t)-\rho(t)-\delta_{\mathrm{model}}.

The true dynamics are

x_{t+1}=F_{\theta^*}(x_t,u_t)+w_t,

where w_t is a bounded disturbance with \|w_t\|\le\delta_{\mathrm{model}}, and \theta^* is the true parameter. By the certified estimator condition,

|\tilde{\theta}_i(t)|=|\theta^*_i-\hat{\theta}_i(t)|\le\rho_i(t),

and the robust margin satisfies

\rho(t)\ge\|\tilde{\theta}(t)\|+\delta_{\mathrm{model}}.

Therefore,

B(x_{t+1})=B(F_{\theta^*}(x_t,u_t)+w_t)
\ge B(F_{\hat{\theta}}(x_t,u_t))-\|\tilde{\theta}(t)\|-\delta_{\mathrm{model}}
\ge(1-\alpha)B(x_t),

where we have used the Lipschitz continuity of B with respect to the parameter and the disturbance bound. Since B(x_t)\ge 0 and \alpha\in(0,1], we have (1-\alpha)B(x_t)\ge 0, so B(x_{t+1})\ge 0.

For the parameter component, the update \hat{\theta}_{t+1}=G(\hat{\theta}_t,x_t,u_t) is designed to maintain \hat{\theta}_{t+1}\in\Theta_{\mathrm{safe}} by the certified estimator condition. Hence (x_{t+1},\hat{\theta}_{t+1})\in\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{joint}}.

By induction, (x_t,\hat{\theta}_t)\in\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{joint}} for all t\ge 0. Since \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{joint}}\subseteq\mathcal{V}\times\Theta_{\mathrm{safe}}, we have x_t\in\mathcal{V} for all t\ge 0. \square

Remark A.3. The robust margin \rho(t) must dominate both the parameter estimation error \|\tilde{\theta}(t)\| and the model mismatch \delta_{\mathrm{model}}. The recent literature on composite adaptive control barrier functions (CaCBF) confirms that a direct coupling between estimation accuracy and the safety margin is necessary to reconcile safety guarantees with parametric uncertainty. The CaCBF approach dynamically reduces conservatism while guaranteeing safety, unlike robust approaches that rely on static worst-case bounds.

