A.4 Distributionally Robust Viability Kernel: Monotonicity and Fixed-Point Characterization (Theorem 11.2)

Theorem (Fixed-Point Characterization of Distributionally Robust Viability). Define the distributionally robust admissibility operator

\Phi_{\mathrm{DR}}(K)=\left\{x\in K:\exists u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}},\ \inf_{P\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{P}_P(F(x,u,w)\in K)=1\right\}.

Then \Phi_{\mathrm{DR}} is monotone on the powerset lattice (\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X}),\subseteq), and its greatest fixed point coincides with the distributionally robust viability kernel:

\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}^{\mathrm{DR}}=\operatorname{gfp}(\Phi_{\mathrm{DR}}).

Proof.

Step 1: Monotonicity. Suppose K_1\subseteq K_2. Take any x\in\Phi_{\mathrm{DR}}(K_1). Then x\in K_1 and there exists u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}} with

\inf_{P\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{P}_P(F(x,u,w)\in K_1)=1.

Since K_1\subseteq K_2, we have F(x,u,w)\in K_1\Rightarrow F(x,u,w)\in K_2 for all w, so

\mathbb{P}_P(F(x,u,w)\in K_2)\ge\mathbb{P}_P(F(x,u,w)\in K_1)=1

for every P\in\mathcal{P}. Hence \inf_{P\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{P}_P(F(x,u,w)\in K_2)=1, and since x\in K_1\subseteq K_2, we have x\in\Phi_{\mathrm{DR}}(K_2).

Step 2: Knaster–Tarski. By the same argument as in Theorem 2.1, \Phi_{\mathrm{DR}} is a monotone operator on the complete lattice \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X}), so it has a greatest fixed point by the Knaster–Tarski theorem.

Step 3: Identification with the distributionally robust viability kernel. Let K^*=\operatorname{gfp}(\Phi_{\mathrm{DR}}). Then K^*=\Phi_{\mathrm{DR}}(K^*), so K^*\subseteq\mathcal{V}, and for every x\in K^* there exists u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}} such that \inf_{P\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{P}_P(F(x,u,w)\in K^*)=1. By induction, for any x_0\in K^*, we can construct a sequence u_0,u_1,\dots such that for all P\in\mathcal{P},

\mathbb{P}_P(x_t\in K^*\ \forall t\ge 0)=1.

Thus x_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}^{\mathrm{DR}}.

Conversely, if x_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}^{\mathrm{DR}}, then there exists a sequence u_0,u_1,\dots such that for all P\in\mathcal{P}, \mathbb{P}_P(x_t\in\mathcal{V}\ \forall t\ge 0)=1. Define S=\{x_t(\omega):t\ge 0,\ \omega\in\Omega\} where \Omega is the sample space. Then S\subseteq\mathcal{V}, and for every x_t\in S, F(x_t,u_t,w)\in S with probability 1 under every P\in\mathcal{P}. Hence S\subseteq\Phi_{\mathrm{DR}}(S), and by the greatest fixed point characterization, S\subseteq\operatorname{gfp}(\Phi_{\mathrm{DR}}). Therefore x_0\in\operatorname{gfp}(\Phi_{\mathrm{DR}}). \square

Remark A.4. The robust viability kernel for discrete-time switched systems with bounded disturbance has been computed via the robust one-step set and Pontryagin difference, with convergence of the iterative algorithm proved using a null controllable set. The distributionally robust version replaces the hard constraint over bounded disturbances with a probabilistic constraint over an ambiguity set of distributions.

