A.6 Attractor Identification under Partial Observability (Theorem 6.1)

Theorem (Attractor Identification). Under detectability, basin coverage, and finite attractor ambiguity, there exists N(\delta) such that for N\ge N(\delta):

\mathbb{P}\left(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{unsafe}}\subseteq\widehat{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathrm{unsafe}}\mid z_{0:N}\right)\ge 1-\delta.

Proof sketch. The proof combines three ingredients.

Ingredient 1: Basin coverage. The exploration policy ensures that every relevant basin \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_i) is visited with positive probability. Formally, for every basin of interest, there exists t\ge 0 with

\mathbb{P}(x_t\in\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_i))>0.

Let p_i=\min_{0\le t\le T}\mathbb{P}(x_t\in\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_i)) for a sufficiently long exploration window T. By the basin coverage assumption, p_i>0 for all relevant basins.

Ingredient 2: Detectability. For any two attractors \mathcal{A}_i\neq\mathcal{A}_j, there exists an observation sequence distinguishing them. Let \epsilon_{\mathrm{det}}>0 be the minimum separation in observation space between distinct attractors.

Ingredient 3: Finite ambiguity. The number of attractors is finite and bounded by K. This bounds the covering number of the attractor set.

Now consider the N observations z_{0:N}. By basin coverage, each basin \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A}_i) is visited at least once with probability at least 1-(1-p_i)^N. By detectability, once a basin is visited, the corresponding attractor is identified with probability at least 1-\epsilon_{\mathrm{det}}. By a union bound over the K attractors,

\mathbb{P}\left(\text{all attractors identified}\right)\ge 1-K\left((1-p_{\min})^N+\epsilon_{\mathrm{det}}\right),

where p_{\min}=\min_i p_i. Setting

N(\delta)=\left\lceil\frac{\log\left(\frac{K}{2\delta}\right)}{-\log(1-p_{\min})}\right\rceil,

we obtain

\mathbb{P}\left(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{unsafe}}\subseteq\widehat{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathrm{unsafe}}\mid z_{0:N}\right)\ge 1-\delta.

This completes the proof sketch. \square

Remark A.5. The basin coverage assumption is strictly weaker than per-basin ergodicity. Ergodicity on each basin ensures that, conditional on entering a basin, the trajectory visits all of it with positive frequency. It does not ensure that the trajectory ever enters every basin. A single trajectory initialized in one basin may never visit another. Without an exploration or repeated-initialization mechanism, identification of all attractors cannot be guaranteed. Recent work on RNN attractor reconstruction under partial observation shows that latent Jacobian ODE models recover the Kaplan–Yorke dimension of chaotic attractors, indicating that attractor topology is preserved under partial observation. However, partial observation can induce spurious attractor structure in surrogate models, highlighting the need for careful identifiability analysis.

