Appendix B: Validation of Version 4.0 Corrections Against the Literature

B.1 CLF–CBF Compatibility as a Common-Control Condition

Correction. Strict CLF–CBF compatibility requires

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CLF}}(x)\cap\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(x)\cap\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}\neq\varnothing

for every x\in\mathcal{C}.

Literature validation. The IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control paper "Converse Theorems for Certificates of Safety and Stability" explicitly shows via a counterexample that the existence of a CLF and a CBF does not imply the existence of a strictly compatible CLF–CBF pair. The companion paper "A Converse Control Lyapunov Theorem for Joint Safety and Stability" establishes that the existence of a strictly compatible pair is equivalent to the existence of a single smooth Lyapunov function certifying both asymptotic stability and safety. This confirms the correction is mathematically precise and closes a genuine loophole.

B.2 Discrete-Time CLF Decrease Condition

Correction. The discrete-time CLF condition requires \alpha(s)\le s on the relevant range of V.

Literature validation. The standard discrete-time CBF condition takes the form where \alpha(\cdot) is a class-\mathcal{K} function satisfying \alpha(s)\le s for all s\ge 0. This is standard in discrete-time Lyapunov theory: without this constraint, the decrease condition V(F(x,u))\le V(x)-\alpha(V(x)) can force V(F(x,u))<0, contradicting the non-negativity of V.

B.3 Discrete-Time CBF Condition

Correction. The discrete-time CBF condition is B(F(x,u))\ge(1-\alpha)B(x) with \alpha\in(0,1].

Literature validation. The discrete-time CBF literature confirms that for \alpha\in(0,1), the system is said to be (1-\alpha)-safe if it satisfies the barrier condition with this form. The condition \alpha\in[0,1] is used in risk-aware discrete-time CBFs, and the standard form for relative degree one systems is exactly B(F(x,u))\ge(1-\alpha)B(x).

B.4 Hamilton–Jacobi Sign Convention

Correction. The viability kernel is the sublevel set \operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}=\{x:V(x)\le 0\}, consistent with the Hamilton–Jacobi–Isaacs equation with the \max\{-V\} term enforcing the boundary condition.

Literature validation. Within a Hamilton–Jacobi framework, the viability kernel is characterized as the zero sublevel set of a value function satisfying a constrained Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman equation in the viscosity sense. The sign of the Laplacian flips with the direction of time, and negative signs in the Hamiltonian arise from the backward-time transformation and the min-over-max structure of reachability propagation.

B.5 Robust Margin for Adaptive Safety

Correction. The robust margin must satisfy \rho(t)\ge\|\tilde{\theta}(t)\|+\delta_{\mathrm{model}}.

Literature validation. The robust adaptive discrete-time CBF literature establishes a barrier function certificate in discrete time for general online parameter estimation algorithms, guaranteeing positive invariance of the safe set despite disturbances and parametric uncertainty without access to the true system parameters. The composite adaptive CBF (CaCBF) framework creates a direct coupling between estimation accuracy and safety margin, dynamically reducing conservatism while guaranteeing safety.

B.6 Basin Coverage for Attractor Identification

Correction. Per-basin ergodicity is insufficient; basin coverage is required.

Literature validation. The exploration policy must ensure that every relevant basin is visited with positive probability. Recent work on attractor reconstruction from partial data shows that perturbations can provide both additional state-space coverage and the input excitation needed to constrain quantities, whereas passive observations primarily constrain the unforced landscape near the occupied wells. The impossibility of internal attractor basin detection under mild structural assumptions further supports the need for explicit coverage guarantees.

