Appendix C: Corrected and New Proofs for Version 4.0

C.1 Proof of the Common-Control Condition for CLF–CBF Compatibility

Proposition C.1. If a CLF V and CBF B are strictly compatible in the sense of Definition 4.1, then the quadratic program

\begin{aligned}
\min_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\quad & \|u-u_{\mathrm{ref}}\|^2\\
\text{s.t.}\quad
& V(F(x,u))-V(x)\le-\alpha(V(x)),\\
& B(F(x,u))-B(x)\ge-\gamma(B(x)),\\
& u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}.
\end{aligned}

is feasible for every x\in\mathcal{C}.

Proof. By Definition 4.1, for every x\in\mathcal{C} there exists u^*\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}} such that

u^*\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CLF}}(x)\cap\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(x)\cap\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}.

This u^* satisfies both the CLF decrease inequality and the CBF invariance inequality. Setting u=u^* in the QP yields a feasible point, since the objective \|u-u_{\mathrm{ref}}\|^2 is finite for any finite u_{\mathrm{ref}}. The constraint set is nonempty, and the QP admits a solution (since \mathcal{U} is compact). \square

C.2 Proof of the Wasserstein Duality Reformulation (Proposition 11.1)

Proposition C.2 (Wasserstein Duality Reformulation). Under Lipschitz continuity of the barrier function and bounded support, the distributionally robust barrier condition

\inf_{P\in\mathbb{B}_q(\hat{\mathbb{P}}_N,\rho)}\mathbb{P}\left(B(F(x,u,w))\ge(1-\alpha)B(x)\right)\ge 1-\delta

admits a tractable reformulation as a finite-dimensional convex program via Wasserstein duality.

Proof sketch. The proof follows the standard duality argument for Wasserstein distributionally robust optimization.

Define the unsafe event

\mathcal{E}=\{w:B(F(x,u,w))<(1-\alpha)B(x)\}.

The worst-case probability over the Wasserstein ball is

\inf_{P\in\mathbb{B}_q(\hat{\mathbb{P}}_N,\rho)}\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E}).

By the duality of Wasserstein DRO, this can be expressed as

\inf_{P\in\mathbb{B}_q(\hat{\mathbb{P}}_N,\rho)}\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})=\sup_{\lambda\ge 0}\left\{1-\lambda\rho-\mathbb{E}_{\hat{\mathbb{P}}_N}[\phi_\lambda(w)]\right\},

where \phi_\lambda is the Moreau envelope of the indicator function of the unsafe event:

\phi_\lambda(w)=\sup_{\bar{w}}\left\{\mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{E}}(\bar{w})-\lambda d_{\mathcal{Y}}(w,\bar{w})^q\right\}.

Under Lipschitz continuity of the barrier function B (with Lipschitz constant L_B) and bounded support, the Moreau envelope admits a closed-form expression. Specifically, for q=1, we have

\phi_\lambda(w)=\max\left\{0,\ 1-\lambda\cdot d(w,\partial\mathcal{E})\right\},

where d(w,\partial\mathcal{E}) is the distance from w to the boundary of the unsafe event. Substituting this into the dual expression yields

\inf_{P\in\mathbb{B}_q(\hat{\mathbb{P}}_N,\rho)}\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})=\sup_{\lambda\ge 0}\left\{1-\lambda\rho-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N\max\left\{0,\ 1-\lambda\cdot d(w_i,\partial\mathcal{E})\right\}\right\}.

This is a finite-dimensional convex program in the scalar variable \lambda. The constraint \inf_{P}\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})\le\delta is equivalent to

\sup_{\lambda\ge 0}\left\{1-\lambda\rho-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N\max\left\{0,\ 1-\lambda\cdot d(w_i,\partial\mathcal{E})\right\}\right\}\le\delta.

For q=2, the Moreau envelope is the squared distance transform, and the resulting optimization problem is a second-order cone program (SOCP). For general q, the problem is a semidefinite program (SDP). This completes the proof sketch. \square

Remark C.1. Finite-sample guarantees for Wasserstein DRO without the curse of dimensionality have been established via a new concentration inequality controlling the decay rate of large deviation probabilities by the variation of the loss. This provides the statistical foundation for the ambiguity radius \rho in the distributionally robust viability kernel.

C.3 Proof of the Safety of Algorithm 11.1 (Theorem 11.5)

Theorem C.3. Under the assumptions of Theorem 11.3 and Proposition 11.1, if the computational budget C_t is sufficient to solve the QP to feasibility, then Algorithm 11.1 guarantees x_t\in\mathcal{V} for all t\ge 0 with probability at least 1-\delta.

Proof. The proof combines the distributionally robust barrier condition with the adaptive horizon guarantee.

At each step t, the algorithm solves the distributionally robust QP:

\begin{aligned}
\min_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\quad & \|u-u_{\mathrm{ref}}\|^2\\
\text{s.t.}\quad
& V(F_{\hat{\theta}}(x_t,u))-V(x_t)\le-\alpha(V(x_t)),\\
& B(F_{\hat{\theta}}(x_t,u))\ge(1-\alpha)B(x_t)-\rho(t)-\delta_{\mathrm{model}},\\
& u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}.
\end{aligned}

By Proposition 11.1, the distributionally robust barrier condition is equivalent to a tractable convex program, and the QP is feasible whenever the state is in the distributionally robust viability kernel \operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}^{\mathrm{DR}}.

The robust margin \rho(t)+\delta_{\mathrm{model}} dominates the worst-case parameter error and model mismatch, so the CBF inequality holds for the true system whenever it holds for the estimated system. By Theorem 11.3, forward invariance of \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{joint}} follows by induction on t. The adaptive horizon guarantees that the computational budget is allocated where it is most needed: when the safety margin m_t is small or the marginal value \mathcal{M}\text{-Val} is large, the horizon N is increased and the ambiguity radius \rho is reduced by collecting more samples.

Since the QP is feasible at every step (by the distributionally robust viability kernel assumption) and the robust margin dominates the uncertainty, the closed-loop system satisfies x_t\in\mathcal{V} for all t\ge 0 with probability at least 1-\delta. \square

